            "pageCount": 1,
            "processed": true,
            "summary": "The article discusses a case involving a 71-year-old woman who experienced dizziness and a fall after starting Sedaprin, a medication prescribed for sleep difficulties. The woman began taking 10mg of Sedaprin nightly, but just two days after starting the medication, she felt dizzy when standing and subsequently fell in her bathroom, resulting in a hip bruise. Following this incident, the decision was made to discontinue Sedaprin, and the woman did not experience any further falls in the month following the discontinuation.\n\nThis document is relevant to a patient-safety review because it highlights a potential adverse reaction (dizziness leading to a fall) associated with the use of Sedaprin in an elderly patient. The case raises important considerations regarding the safety and risks of prescribing this medication to older adults, who may be more susceptible to side effects. It also presents an opportunity for examining medication protocols and monitoring practices for elderly patients starting new medications. Overall, the case underscores the need for careful evaluation of drug safety, particularly in vulnerable populations.",
